<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 39 M1, AC M1.5/M1.6. The `assert-envelope` verb, over the SHIPPED
  inline renderers.

  preview_envelope_test (ctest) gates the collect SEAM against the DRAWN pixels,
  but it has to build its leaf renderer as a fixture: a real SPlainWave needs
  setWave(), which needs an SAppContext with a current project and a decodable
  file. This case is the other half - a real project, a real ../test_sawtooth.wav,
  a real SCut, and therefore the real SCutRendererInline -> SPlainWaveRendererInline
  delegation, driven exactly as a script will drive it from M2 onward.

  WHAT IT PINS

  1. The verb produces an envelope AT ALL from a clip index-path, and the
     envelope is DETERMINISTIC: the same window collected twice is
     BYTE-IDENTICAL. That is the snapshot/compareTo pair, and it is the whole
     mechanism M2 needs to assert "moving the fader did not move one byte of
     the waveform" without hard-coding a single expected probe.

  2. THE FIXTURE'S OWN SHAPE IS VISIBLE IN THE PROBES. ../test_sawtooth.wav is
     a RAMPED sawtooth (testkit CONTRACT inv. 3): its amplitude grows linearly
     across the four seconds. A 4-column probe of the whole clip therefore reads
     0 / 25 / 50 / 76 - a straight line through the origin, ~25.4 per column,
     which is the ramp itself and not a measurement anyone had to tune. A
     collect reading the wrong part of the clip, or the same part four times,
     cannot produce it. Without this the case would be self-referential:
     comparing an array to itself passes on all-zeros.

  3. An EVENT clip contributes NOTHING. SMidiCut has no waveform, so its
     renderer inherits SObjectRenderer::collectEnvelope's `false` - the M1.4
     property, at script level. `expectEmpty` is how a caller says so.

  NOT gated here: pixels (preview_envelope_test does that), the volume
  independence (that is M2's preview_volume_independent.qxa - TODAY the drawn
  waveform IS still scaled by the fader, deliberately, so that M1 is verifiable
  as behaviour-preserving), and mode="childSum" (M3).
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="envelope_probe">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- The whole 4-second clip in four columns, one per source second. The
         ramp makes each one taller than the last, in a straight line: column 0
         is the silence the ramp starts from, and 1/2/3 are 25 / 50 / 76.
         tolerance defaults to 0 - the probes are integers. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="192000" width="4"
                     column="1" min="-25" max="25"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="192000" width="4"
                     column="2" min="-50" max="50"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="192000" width="4"
                     column="3" min="-76" max="76"/>

    <!-- Deterministic: the same window, collected again, is byte-identical.
         64 columns, so this compares 128 bytes rather than a lucky few. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="192000" width="64"
                     snapshot="whole"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="0" length="192000" width="64"
                     compareTo="whole"/>

    <!-- A DIFFERENT window is a different array, or compareTo would pass on
         anything and prove nothing. -->
    <assert-envelope clip="0,0" start="96000" length="96000" width="64"
                     compareTo="whole" expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- An event clip has no waveform: the renderer declines. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="1" timePos="0" duration="7680" name="Events"/>
    <add-note clip="1,0" tick="0" dur="960" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <assert-envelope clip="1,0" start="0" length="192000" width="16"
                     expectEmpty="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
